WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.5 · Financial Management
Cash flow, forecasting and runway

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Start-up costs come first and income later: forecast your cash monthly and know your runway.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:27.763
A cash flow forecast shows, month by month, the cash you expect to receive and pay out, and what is left. Runway is how many months you could keep going if no new income arrived.

4
00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:28.763
How the lines connect

5
00:00:28.763 --> 00:00:37.870
Opening balance: Cash in the bank at the start of the month. For example: The cooperative starts January with 4,000 in its bank account.

6
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:49.200
Receipts and payments: Money actually arriving and leaving, not money invoiced or promised. For example: In: 6,500 from sales, a client and a grant. Out: 5,300 for pay, rent and suppliers.

7
00:00:49.200 --> 00:01:00.159
Closing balance: What is left at the end of the month; it becomes next month’s opening balance. For example: 4,000 + 6,500 − 5,300 leaves 5,200 to start February.

8
00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:10.467
Runway: Closing balance divided by your average monthly payments. For example: 5,200 ÷ 4,800 a month gives just over one month of runway.

9
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:11.467
Why cash comes first

10
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:16.770
1. A surplus on paper does not pay bills; cash does.

11
00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:22.444
2. Start-up costs come first and income later, so gaps are likely.

12
00:01:22.444 --> 00:01:29.119
3. It shows a shortfall months ahead, while you can still act.

13
00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:30.119
How you will use it

14
00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:35.422
1. Monthly: replace estimates with actual figures and check your runway.

15
00:01:35.422 --> 00:01:41.096
2. Scenarios: test late sales, late payments or a failed funding bid.

16
00:01:41.096 --> 00:01:47.770
3. Triggers: a minimum runway that sets off actions agreed in advance.

17
00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:48.770
What to avoid

18
00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:53.704
Counting invoices as cash before the money actually arrives.

19
00:01:53.704 --> 00:01:58.267
Building the forecast once and never updating it.

20
00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:05.311
Waiting for a crisis to decide what you would delay or cut.

21
00:02:05.911 --> 00:02:07.711
You draft a twelve-month cash forecast and set your runway trigger.

22
00:02:07.711 --> 00:02:11.711
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:13.211 --> 00:02:16.311
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
